A hash map — `dict` in Python, `Map` in JavaScript, `unordered_map` in C++ — stores key → value pairs and finds a key in O(1) time on average. It turns the key into a number (its *hash*) and uses that number to jump straight to a bucket instead of searching.

That turns "have I seen this before?" from a scan into a single lookup, and it is the most common way to take an O(n²) array solution down to O(n).

## The seen-set pattern

Walk the array once. Before handling an element, ask a set whether it has appeared; afterwards, add it:

```python run
def has_duplicate(nums):
    seen = set()
    for x in nums:
        if x in seen:      # O(1) on average
            return True
        seen.add(x)
    return False

print(has_duplicate([3, 1, 4, 1, 5]))  # True
print(has_duplicate([2, 7, 1, 8]))     # False
```

## Two sum: look up the partner

To find two indices whose values add up to `target`, notice that each element `x` needs exactly one partner value:

$$
\text{need} = \text{target} - x
$$

So instead of pairing `x` with every other element, look `need` up in a map of values already seen (value → index). Step through it:

:::viz{id=hash-map}

```javascript run
function twoSum(nums, target) {
  const seen = new Map(); // value -> index
  for (let i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
    const need = target - nums[i];
    if (seen.has(need)) return [seen.get(need), i];
    seen.set(nums[i], i);
  }
  return [];
}

console.log(twoSum([4, 9, 1, 12, 6, 3], 9)); // [ 4, 5 ]
console.log(twoSum([3, 3], 6));              // [ 0, 1 ]
```

:::callout{kind=pitfall title="Look up before you insert"}
Check for the partner *before* storing the current element. Store first and `x` can pair with itself: with target 6, a single 3 would "find" its own index.
:::

:::callout{kind=complexity time="O(n)" space="O(n)"}
One pass with an O(1) average lookup per element. The price is memory: the map may end up holding every element. Lookups only degrade toward O(n) when many keys collide, which a good hash function makes very unlikely.
:::

:::question{slug=two-sum}

When an array is sorted, its two ends hold the smallest and largest values. Two indices — one at each end, moving toward each other — can then search pairs in a single pass, because every comparison tells you which end can be thrown away for good.

## Pair sum on a sorted array

To find two values that add up to `target`, start with `l` at the first index and `r` at the last:

- if `a[l] + a[r]` is **too big**, `a[r]` is too big even with the smallest partner still available, so move `r` left;
- if it is **too small**, `a[l]` is too small even with the largest partner still available, so move `l` right;
- if it **matches**, you are done.

:::viz{id=two-pointers}

```python run
def pair_sum(a, target):
    l, r = 0, len(a) - 1
    while l < r:
        s = a[l] + a[r]
        if s == target:
            return l, r
        if s > target:
            r -= 1   # a[r] cannot pair with anything left
        else:
            l += 1   # a[l] cannot pair with anything left
    return None

print(pair_sum([1, 3, 4, 6, 8, 11, 15], 10))   # (2, 3)
print(pair_sum([1, 3, 4, 6, 8, 11, 15], 100))  # None
```

Each step discards one element, so the loop runs at most n − 1 times.

:::callout{kind=complexity time="O(n)" space="O(1)"}
Linear time and no extra memory — the hash-map approach needs O(n) memory. The catch: the input must be sorted, and sorting first costs O(n log n).
:::

## Palindromes

A string is a palindrome if it reads the same both ways. Compare the outermost pair, then move both pointers inward, skipping anything that is not a letter or digit:

```python run
def is_palindrome(s):
    l, r = 0, len(s) - 1
    while l < r:
        if not s[l].isalnum():
            l += 1
        elif not s[r].isalnum():
            r -= 1
        elif s[l].lower() != s[r].lower():
            return False
        else:
            l, r = l + 1, r - 1
    return True

print(is_palindrome("Never odd or even"))  # True
print(is_palindrome("two pointers"))       # False
```

:::callout{kind=pitfall title="< not <="}
Loop while `l < r`. When the pointers meet, one element is left and it cannot pair with itself — in pair sum, `l <= r` would happily count `a[i] + a[i]`.
:::

:::question{slug=valid-palindrome}

:::question{slug=reverse-string}

The second two-pointer shape moves both pointers in the *same* direction. A fast **read** pointer visits every element; a slow **write** pointer marks where the next kept element goes. Everything before `write` is the finished answer, so the array is filtered in place with no extra memory.

## Filtering in place

Remove every zero while keeping the other elements in order:

```python run
def remove_zeros(a):
    write = 0
    for read in range(len(a)):
        if a[read] != 0:
            a[write] = a[read]
            write += 1
    return a[:write]

print(remove_zeros([0, 1, 0, 3, 12]))  # [1, 3, 12]
```

The same shape removes duplicates from a sorted array: keep an element only when it differs from the last one kept.

```javascript run
function dedupeSorted(a) {
  let write = 0;
  for (let read = 0; read < a.length; read++) {
    if (write === 0 || a[read] !== a[write - 1]) a[write++] = a[read];
  }
  return a.slice(0, write);
}

console.log(dedupeSorted([1, 1, 2, 3, 3, 3, 7])); // [ 1, 2, 3, 7 ]
```

## Three sum: fix one, then two pointers

To list every triple that sums to zero, sort, fix the first element `nums[i]`, and run the pair search on the rest looking for `-nums[i]`. Sorting also makes duplicates adjacent, so repeated triples are easy to skip:

```python run
def three_sum(nums):
    nums = sorted(nums)
    out = []
    for i in range(len(nums) - 2):
        if i > 0 and nums[i] == nums[i - 1]:
            continue                      # same first element, same triples
        l, r = i + 1, len(nums) - 1
        while l < r:
            s = nums[i] + nums[l] + nums[r]
            if s < 0:
                l += 1
            elif s > 0:
                r -= 1
            else:
                out.append([nums[i], nums[l], nums[r]])
                l += 1
                while l < r and nums[l] == nums[l - 1]:
                    l += 1                # skip repeated second elements
    return out

print(three_sum([-1, 0, 1, 2, -1, -4]))  # [[-1, -1, 2], [-1, 0, 1]]
```

Fixing each element costs one linear pair search, so the sort is not the bottleneck:

$$
T(n) = O(n \log n) + \sum_{i=0}^{n-3} O(n - i) = O(n^2)
$$

:::callout{kind=pitfall title="Duplicates"}
Without the two skip rules, `[-1, 0, 1, 2, -1, -4]` reports `[-1, 0, 1]` twice. Skip a repeated first element, and after each hit skip repeated second elements.
:::

:::callout{kind=note title="Challenge: trapping rain water"}
Two pointers also solve *trapping rain water*: keep the tallest bar seen from each side, and always advance the side whose tallest bar is lower — the water above that bar is limited by that side alone.
:::

:::question{slug=three-sum}

:::question{slug=trapping-rain-water}

A stack only lets you touch its top: `push` puts an item on top, `pop` takes the top item off. The last thing in is the first thing out. A Python list is a stack (`append` and `pop` at the end are O(1)), so is a JavaScript array; in C++ use `std::stack` or a `std::vector`.

Stacks model anything that must be undone in reverse order: nested brackets, function calls (the call stack), the back button, an undo history.

## Matching brackets

Every closing bracket must match the most recent opening bracket that is still unmatched — which is exactly the top of a stack. Step through a string that goes wrong at the very end:

:::viz{id=bracket-stack}

```python run
PAIRS = {")": "(", "]": "[", "}": "{"}

def is_valid(s):
    stack = []
    for ch in s:
        if ch in PAIRS:                  # a closer
            if not stack or stack.pop() != PAIRS[ch]:
                return False
        else:                            # an opener
            stack.append(ch)
    return not stack                     # leftovers were never closed

for s in ["{[()()]}", "([)]", "((", "{[()()]}(]"]:
    print(f"{s:12} {is_valid(s)}")
```

:::callout{kind=pitfall title="Two ways to fail"}
A string is invalid if a closer meets the wrong opener (or an empty stack) — and *also* if openers are left over at the end: `"(("` never hits a mismatch. Forgetting the final `not stack` check is the most common bug here.
:::

:::callout{kind=complexity time="O(n)" space="O(n)"}
Every character is pushed or popped once. The stack can hold up to n openers.
:::

